"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings are favorable, showing negative surgical margins and no invasion of the seminal vesicles, which are positive prognostic indicators. Conversely, the preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.1, and the biopsy revealed Gleason 3+4 disease with perineural invasion. The absence of lymph node metastasis is also a positive finding.\n\nThe nodal status is documented as negative, which supports a better outcome. However, the biopsy findings, specifically the presence of perineural invasion, represent a finding that may influence recurrence risk. The difference between the biopsy and prostatectomy findings is noted, though the surgical report details the pathology of the specimen. Postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory are critical missing pieces of information that would significantly revise this prognosis. Missing follow-up data prevents a definitive assessment of the current state. The fixed estimated horizon is 57.51 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 46.01–69.02 months; its coverage has not been validated."
